  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py`, and the full test suite passes: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` ran 13 tests, OK.\n\n```python\nif parts < 1:\n    raise ValueError(f\"parts must be >= 1: {parts}\")\nbase, extra = divmod(cents, parts)\nreturn [base + 1 if i < extra else base for i in range(parts)]\n```\n\n- **Tests:** there was no money test file, so I created `tests/test_money.py` in the same unittest style as `tests/test_pricing.py`. It checks:\n  - an even split\n  - the remainder going to the first elements (`split(10, 3) == [4, 3, 3]`)\n  - the parts summing to `cents`\n  - `parts=1` and `cents=0`\n  - `ValueError` when `parts` is 0 or negative\n- **Negative `cents`:** the parts still add up correctly, but the extra cent goes on the *last* elements: `split(-10, 3)` returns `[-3, -3, -4]`. This is because Python's floor division rounds negative numbers down. Your spec didn't cover negatives, so I left it. If refunds or other negative amounts should split the same way as positive ones (`[-4, -3, -3]`), I can split the absolute value and negate the result.\n\nNothing is committed.",
